export const debate = {"id":"oconnor-biddle-objective-morality-2024","number":"23","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","assessmentRubric":"Slugfester Reassessment Rubric v2","title":"Alex O'Connor vs Craig Biddle: Is Morality Objective?","label":"Objectivist morality and emotivism","date":"2026-08-28","duration":"1 hr 33 min","youtubeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A4JGJRmldQE","motion":"Is morality objectively grounded in the factual requirements of human life, or are moral claims expressions of emotion without truth value?","sourceNote":"Based on the complete debate transcript. Summaries are condensed for clarity, and quotations are kept brief and faithful to the source.","scoringNote":"Scores are AI-generated estimates of argumentative performance and are intended as transparent, revisable judgments rather than final verdicts.","summary":"Biddle grounds objective morality in life and flourishing; O'Connor argues that conditional valuation cannot establish objective duties, especially toward other people and animals.","quotes":{"pro":{"text":"life is the root, the ultimate foundation of the concept of value","context":"Biddle identifies living organisms' conditional requirements as the factual source and ultimate standard of evaluative concepts."},"con":{"text":"ethical statements are expressions of emotion","context":"O'Connor distinguishes emotivism from subjectivism by denying that ethical utterances report truth-apt propositions."}},"sides":{"pro":{"name":"Objectivist morality","speaker":"Craig Biddle","color":"teal"},"con":{"name":"Emotivist challenge","speaker":"Alex O'Connor","color":"coral"}},"score":{"pro":82,"con":89},"sections":[{"sectionId":"section-moral-meaning-and-objectivity","title":"Moral Meaning, Emotion, and Objective Reference","timebox":"1:19–60:28","score":{"pro":83,"con":85},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-objectivity-mind-fact-relation","time":"14:46","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Objectivity is a cognitive mind-fact relationship grounded in life-relevant conditions, without which evaluative concepts lose their factual basis.","score":83,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply avoids treating goodness as a mysterious substance by construing objectivity relationally and connecting evaluative cognition to publicly investigable facts concerning life's requirements. Principal limitation: The term objective alternates between epistemic correspondence and moral status, while the argument does not establish that moral predicates report life-facts rather than expressing attitudes toward independently cognizable conditions. Live burden: The account must specify moral truth conditions, explain normative authority, and compare its proposed life-reference with emotivist semantics instead of assuming that factual cognition automatically determines moral meaning. Locked score: The supplied strong-and-competent band reflects excellent motion relevance, direct responsiveness, and a recoverable naturalistic proposal while preserving the important semantic gap and limited defense of life's alleged exclusivity.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-emotivist-meaning","time":"1:19","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Emotivism holds that ethical statements express non-propositional emotions rather than reporting objective facts or truth-valued personal preferences.","score":90,"critique":"Strongest feature: The opening carefully differentiates emotivism from truth-apt subjectivism, supplies a stable semantic proposition, and directly establishes the challenge's motion-level alternative with commendable conceptual precision. Principal limitation: It announces an internally coherent interpretation of ethical discourse without yet providing substantial linguistic, psychological, or comparative evidence showing that ordinary moral assertions actually function this way. Live burden: The account must explain disagreement, deliberation, inference, correction, and apparently factual moral reasoning while demonstrating that emotional expression handles those practices better than truth-conditional competitors. Locked score: The supplied very-strong band appropriately reflects exceptional relevance, recoverability, and coherence, alongside a narrower evidential limitation and the understandable absence of developed engagement in an introductory constructive statement.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-emotions-not-moral-knowledge","time":"59:37","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Emotions register existing values but do not produce knowledge; rational inquiry identifies life as the objective standard governing moral evaluation.","score":83,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply cleanly distinguishes affective registration from cognitive justification and offers rational investigation of life-relevant facts as the proposed epistemic alternative to emotional expression. Principal limitation: Showing that emotions are not knowledge-producing faculties does not establish that moral utterances describe facts, and life's status as the objective standard receives limited additional warrant here. Live burden: The account must connect rationally discovered biological and psychological facts to moral truth conditions, while addressing the emotivist possibility that factual reasoning informs attitudes without transforming them into propositions. Locked score: The supplied strong-and-competent band appropriately recognizes conceptual clarity, direct responsiveness, stable terminology, and important motion relevance while preserving the semantic non sequitur and underdeveloped defense of the selected standard.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-objective-oughtness-burden","time":"5:56","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"A materialist objectivist must explain what objective oughtness consists in and how it originates if moral predicates do not express emotion.","score":79,"critique":"Strongest feature: The challenge identifies an indispensable explanatory burden for naturalistic objectivism by asking what objective oughtness is, how it originates, and why it possesses normative authority. Principal limitation: Its spatial language misleadingly suggests that every materialistically respectable property must occupy a simple location, overlooking relations, functions, dispositions, and higher-level natural properties without discrete coordinates. Live burden: The objectivist still must specify truth conditions and authority, while the challenger must show why relational or functional naturalism cannot satisfy those requirements without reifying goodness as an object. Locked score: The supplied strong-and-competent band captures the move's central relevance and sharp explanatory pressure while recognizing partly unstable terminology, compressed ontology, and an unnecessarily restrictive characterization of material existence.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-life-as-value-foundation","title":"Life, Choice, and the Foundation of Value","timebox":"8:52–41:08","score":{"pro":86,"con":90},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-life-living-organisms-found-value","time":"8:52","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Living organisms must pursue sustaining conditions, so life supplies the factual foundation and practical necessity of value and evaluative concepts.","score":83,"critique":"Strongest feature: The argument gives evaluation a concrete biological foothold by connecting organismic survival requirements, goal-directed activity, and the practical necessity of distinguishing beneficial from destructive conditions. Principal limitation: Moving from values pursued by living organisms to life as the exclusive foundation of every evaluative concept receives limited comparison with preference-based, experiential, social, or pluralistic alternatives. Live burden: The account must demonstrate why biological conditionality establishes not merely one intelligible evaluative framework but the ultimate explanatory standard for human moral appraisal, including interpersonal constraints. Locked score: The supplied strong-and-competent band reflects a clear, relevant, and substantially warranted foundation while preserving the locked concern that its universalizing conclusion exceeds the comparatively limited defense offered against rival foundations.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-immortality-counterexample","time":"17:21","role":"Major direct reply","words":"If immortal agents could still suffer and care about one another, ethical concerns would persist although death and biological survival ceased mattering.","score":86,"critique":"Strongest feature: The hypothetical isolates the necessity claim with unusual clarity by removing possible death while preserving suffering, agency, interpersonal treatment, and recognizable ethical concern. Principal limitation: Immortality need not eliminate life-relevant functioning, injury, frustration, or flourishing, so the scenario may challenge survival-centered formulations more decisively than every broader life-centered account. Live burden: The objectivist must explain whether impaired functioning supplies continuing life-based standards, while the challenger must show that the stipulated concerns remain morally intelligible independently of any such conditions. Locked score: The supplied very-strong band appropriately rewards a precise, relevant, well-calibrated counterexample that directly tests the proposed foundation, while recognizing that the broader conception of life as flourishing offers a substantial qualification requiring further analysis.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-choice-to-live-precedes-value","time":"36:25","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Life is not intrinsically valuable; choosing continued existence precedes evaluation and generates the practical need for values and purposeful action.","score":88,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply avoids intrinsic-value assertions and clearly orders the pre-evaluative choice to live, the resulting need for guidance, and subsequent appraisal of life-serving actions. Principal limitation: Locating the foundation in an optional pre-moral choice makes the resulting reasons conditional and therefore does not by itself establish objective authority over agents who reject that choice. Live burden: The account must explain whether objectivity means conditional truth relative to a chosen end or includes an independently justified requirement to choose life, without equivocating between those importantly different claims. Locked score: The supplied very-strong band reflects unusually clear logical architecture, direct engagement with circularity, and strong internal coherence while preserving the central limitation that practical necessity after commitment is not yet categorical moral normativity.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-premoral-choice-gap","time":"39:48","role":"Major direct reply","words":"A pre-moral choice cannot create objective normativity unless remaining alive is independently required; otherwise subsequent morality stays conditional on that choice.","score":93,"critique":"Strongest feature: The objection formulates a disciplined dilemma: either choosing life has independent objective authority, or every downstream norm remains conditional upon a pre-moral commitment lacking that authority. Principal limitation: It presumes that objective morality must bind agents categorically, leaving less room for accounts treating objective reasons as conditionally true given constitutive features of agency or living action. Live burden: The pro side must specify its intended modality, while the con side must show why constitutive or hypothetical objectivity is inadequate to the motion rather than merely less expansive. Locked score: The supplied very-strong band reflects outstanding precision, calibration, directness, and identification of the debate's central inferential gap while acknowledging a narrower interpretation that preserves factual guidance by proportionately limiting the objectivist conclusion.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-facts-to-ought-and-flourishing","title":"From Life Facts to Oughts and Flourishing","timebox":"12:14–76:58","score":{"pro":83,"con":90},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-oughts-guide-volitional-life","time":"12:14","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Human beings need rational guidance, so oughts identify life-supporting actions and chosen virtues organize the pursuit of survival and flourishing.","score":80,"critique":"Strongest feature: The response gives ought-language an intelligible naturalistic function by connecting volitional agency, factual human requirements, rational guidance, and the organization of chosen values into practical principles. Principal limitation: It moves among survival, flourishing, instrumental guidance, and specifically moral authority without fully explaining why facts supporting a chosen end generate unconditional or objectively binding obligations. Live burden: The account must distinguish hypothetical recommendations for agents already committed to living from categorical moral requirements, then justify any authority claimed over agents who reject or differently interpret that commitment. Locked score: The supplied strong-and-competent band appropriately recognizes direct contact with the ontological challenge, high practical relevance, and meaningful explanatory substance while retaining the unresolved fact-to-authority bridge and partly unstable scope.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-life-value-circularity","time":"35:23","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Condemning contempt for life assumes life's disputed value, so it cannot persuade someone who does not already accept that valuation.","score":91,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply precisely identifies the disputed premise and explains why invoking contempt for life cannot justify life's value to an interlocutor who questions that valuation. Principal limitation: Establishing conceptual dependence between evaluation and life does not itself require respecting every living entity, so the objection diagnoses this particular justification without refuting every possible life-based derivation. Live burden: The pro side must provide non-circular reasons connecting purposeless destruction to human flourishing, while the con side must distinguish fatal circularity from legitimate application of an independently defended standard. Locked score: The supplied very-strong band appropriately recognizes exceptional logical focus, direct responsiveness, stable scope, and decisive burden contact while retaining the qualification that the diagnosis targets an underdeveloped application rather than the entire foundational framework.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-life-means-human-flourishing","time":"70:10","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Human life means flourishing through fully exercised human capacities rather than bare survival, with happiness accompanying but not replacing that standard.","score":85,"critique":"Strongest feature: The clarification explicitly rejects bare biological persistence and identifies human life with full-capacity flourishing, substantially improving the framework's ability to address suffering, agency, and richer practical goods. Principal limitation: Broadening life to flourishing risks semantic expansion unless substantive criteria are independently specified, and the asserted relationship among life, flourishing, and happiness remains comparatively compressed. Live burden: The account must define flourishing non-circularly, derive interpersonal and animal-related implications, and show that the broadened standard answers rather than merely redescribes earlier counterexamples. Locked score: The supplied very-strong band appropriately rewards exceptional clarity, scope control, responsiveness, and charitable engagement while preserving the unresolved authority question and the need for an independently defended specification of flourishing.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-flourishing-semantic-inadequacy","time":"75:17","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Human life or flourishing inadequately defines moral goodness because it omits familiar ethical concern for other people and animals.","score":88,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply separates understanding the proposed referent from accepting it as moral goodness and identifies interpersonal and animal concern as concrete domains testing semantic adequacy. Principal limitation: The appeal to what people ordinarily mean by morality lacks representative linguistic evidence, while a sufficiently social conception of flourishing could potentially include benevolence, justice, and animal protection. Live burden: The pro side must derive those inclusions rather than importing them, and the con side must show that ordinary moral practice favors emotivism rather than merely rejecting this particular naturalistic definition. Locked score: The supplied very-strong band reflects excellent precision, calibration, charity, responsiveness, and central relevance while retaining the need for broader semantic evidence and comparison against richer flourishing accounts.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-egoism-rights-and-moral-agency","title":"Rational Egoism, Rights, and Moral Agency","timebox":"41:06–57:06","score":{"pro":78,"con":92},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-human-life-generates-rights","time":"41:06","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Shared human requirements support universal principles and individual rights because each person has a separate life requiring action on personal judgment.","score":76,"critique":"Strongest feature: The argument broadens the life-based framework from individual guidance to social principles by emphasizing shared human nature, separate lives, personal judgment, and freedom from coercion. Principal limitation: The progression from factual requirements to self-ownership and enforceable rights remains compressed, especially because saying that each life belongs to its person introduces a substantive normative premise. Live burden: The account must derive reciprocal constraints from agency and flourishing without presupposing ownership, then explain why those constraints bind an advantaged violator whose prudential costs are stipulated away. Locked score: The supplied strong-and-competent band recognizes relevance, clarity, and a plausible functional basis for political principles while retaining the decisive interpersonal-warrant gap and the comparatively limited engagement with hard counterexamples.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-advantageous-murder-counterexample","time":"43:31","role":"Major direct reply","words":"A life-valuing murderer who genuinely benefits from killing an innocent person challenges morality grounded solely in the actor's own flourishing.","score":91,"critique":"Strongest feature: The counterexample isolates the interpersonal problem by stipulating innocence, genuine all-things-considered benefit, commitment to living, and separation between the actor's flourishing and the victim's life. Principal limitation: The stipulation is philosophically demanding because sophisticated flourishing theories may treat injustice, corrupted character, or irrational dependence on domination as constitutively disadvantageous rather than contingently costly. Live burden: The objectivist must justify rejecting or absorbing the stipulated benefit without definitional maneuvering, while the challenger must show that the scenario remains coherent under the strongest account of rational flourishing. Locked score: The supplied very-strong band reflects exceptional responsiveness, precision, calibration, and pressure on universal rights derivations while preserving a serious constitutive-flourishing reply that requires explicit resolution.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-rights-protect-rational-freedom","time":"54:51","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Rights recognize humanity's factual need for freedom to exercise rational judgment, while aggressors may forfeit freedoms used to threaten others.","score":79,"critique":"Strongest feature: The response gives rights a concrete function by connecting freedom from force to independent rational judgment, productive action, and the social requirements of characteristically human agency. Principal limitation: A widespread functional need does not automatically generate universal enforceable claims, nor does it explain why an advantaged violator must prioritize another person's freedom over personal benefit. Live burden: The account must supply an interpersonal principle connecting each agent's need for freedom to reciprocal duties and justify forfeiture without assuming the rights framework it seeks to establish. Locked score: The supplied strong-and-competent band reflects clear terminology, substantial relevance, and direct contact with the presupposition challenge while preserving the unresolved universality problem and incomplete response to the stipulated advantageous-violator case.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-rights-presupposition","time":"50:09","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Invoking rights against egoistic wrongdoing assumes disputed moral properties and does not explain why an advantaged egoist must respect another person's rights.","score":92,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply distinguishes asserting that victims possess rights from explaining why those rights normatively constrain an egoistic actor, thereby exposing the precise missing bridge in the derivation. Principal limitation: Functional accounts of rights may begin from reciprocal conditions of agency rather than independent moral properties, so presupposition must be demonstrated instead of inferred merely from rights-language. Live burden: The pro side must derive universal compliance under stipulated advantage, while the con side must address contractarian, constitutive, and institutional explanations that could ground constraints without reifying objective properties. Locked score: The supplied very-strong band appropriately reflects outstanding directness, conceptual precision, calibration, and central burden contact while retaining space for a non-presuppositional functional derivation that the exchange did not conclusively eliminate.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-animals-and-practical-boundaries","title":"Animals, Harm, and Practical Boundaries","timebox":"27:25–81:54","score":{"pro":75,"con":84},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-animal-abuse-anti-life","time":"28:44","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Gratuitous animal abuse is immoral because life has value beyond humanity, and purposeless destruction expresses a life-opposing orientation.","score":70,"critique":"Strongest feature: The response unequivocally condemns gratuitous suffering and attempts to extend the life standard beyond narrowly human interests by emphasizing purposeless destructiveness and respect for living beings. Principal limitation: It shifts among biological value, moral wrongness, character assessment, and contempt for life without deriving why an egoistic standard obligates the actor to respect another organism. Live burden: The account must independently connect cruelty toward animals to the agent's flourishing or justify a patient-centered constraint, rather than relying upon the already disputed value of life. Locked score: The supplied mixed band reflects genuine responsiveness, moral clarity, and a potentially productive character-based route while preserving the substantial inferential instability, categorical overstatement, and incomplete answer to the carefully stipulated counterexample.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-animal-torture-counterexample","time":"27:25","role":"Major direct reply","words":"A survival-based egoistic ethic appears unable to condemn recreational animal torture that neither harms the torturer nor frustrates that person's preferences.","score":87,"critique":"Strongest feature: The counterexample sharply tests actor-centered egoism through a carefully stipulated case of gratuitous animal torture lacking survival costs, preference frustration, or obvious prudential disadvantages. Principal limitation: Its force depends on excluding indirect character, psychological, social, and dispositional consequences, and it pressures the proposed derivation more directly than it independently establishes emotivist semantics. Live burden: The objectivist must derive condemnation without importing disputed other-regarding premises, while the challenger must defend the stipulation against accounts connecting cruelty to the agent's rational capacities or flourishing. Locked score: The supplied very-strong band reflects exceptional precision, responsiveness, calibration, and burden relevance while preserving the possibility that a richer conception of flourishing could answer the case through independently justified character-based considerations.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-biological-versus-moral-values","time":"80:24","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Biological values apply broadly, whereas moral values arise through human choice; valuing animals therefore does not entail granting them human rights.","score":79,"critique":"Strongest feature: The response clearly distinguishes biological value, choice-dependent moral agency, human deliberation, and rights, providing a structured explanation for different evaluative treatment across species. Principal limitation: Lack of moral agency does not independently entail lack of moral-patient status, because sentience, interests, vulnerability, or capacity for suffering could ground protections or rights. Live burden: The account must distinguish possessing duties from being owed consideration and then justify the categorical denial of animal rights without relying solely on animals' inability to choose moral principles. Locked score: The supplied strong-and-competent band recognizes stable terminology, meaningful responsiveness, and a useful domain distinction while preserving the insufficient inference from nonagency to rightlessness and the incomplete patient-centered analysis.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-emotive-boundary-explanation","time":"60:39","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Emotional resemblance, attachment, and inherited taboos may explain differential moral concern more naturally than rational-egoist derivations explain those boundaries.","score":81,"critique":"Strongest feature: The response offers a psychologically intelligible explanation of graded concern through resemblance, attachment, and taboo formation, thereby giving emotivism practical explanatory content beyond a semantic definition. Principal limitation: It shifts from animal concern to incest taboos without fully controlling scope, and psychological naturalness does not show that rational evaluation contributes no corrective or normative work. Live burden: The challenger must distinguish causal explanation from justification and compare emotivism with hybrid accounts in which emotions generate attitudes that reason subsequently evaluates against independent considerations. Locked score: The supplied strong-and-competent band reflects coherent examples, appropriate calibration, useful comparative pressure, and direct engagement with boundary cases while retaining compressed evidence and an incomplete answer to the epistemic objection.","tags":[]}}]}],"overall":{"pro":{"score":82,"strengths":["Grounded evaluative concepts in the conditional requirements and goal-directed activity characteristic of living organisms.","Explained objective evaluation as a cognitive relationship between human judgment and life-relevant facts rather than as a separate moral substance.","Clarified that the human-life standard concerns full-capacity flourishing, not mere biological persistence.","Connected rights to the practical human need for freedom to exercise independent rational judgment."],"blunders":[{"text":"The account repeatedly moved from life-relevant facts or a prior choice to live toward objective authority without fully deriving a categorical normative bridge.","links":[]},{"text":"The rights argument did not fully explain why reciprocal constraints bind a stipulated advantaged violator rather than merely serving ordinary mutual benefit.","links":[]},{"text":"The animal response distinguished moral agency from biological value but did not establish why nonagents cannot possess patient-based claims or protections.","links":[]}]},"con":{"score":89,"strengths":["Distinguished emotivism from truth-apt subjectivism with a clear and internally stable semantic account.","Formulated the central dilemma between conditional reasons following a choice to live and an independently objective requirement to make that choice.","Used immortality, animal torture, and advantageous murder to test the scope and interpersonal implications of the life-centered standard.","Exposed the difference between asserting rights and explaining why those rights constrain an egoistic violator."],"blunders":[{"text":"The demand that material oughtness be spatially located imposed an unnecessarily restrictive picture of materialistic explanation.","links":[]},{"text":"The psychological naturalness of emotional boundaries did not by itself establish emotivist semantics or eliminate reason's possible corrective role.","links":[]},{"text":"The semantic appeal to familiar moral concern lacked representative evidence about ordinary moral language and practice.","links":[]}]}},"logicalExtension":{"pro":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that objective morality consists of empirically answerable principles governing the flourishing of rational, socially interdependent agents whose agency requires reciprocal protection.","premises":["Living rational agents possess constitutive needs for cognition, purposive action, bodily integrity, cooperation, and long-term planning, and frustration of these needs predictably impairs their flourishing.","Because human flourishing depends upon stable interaction with other independent agents, principles authorizing opportunistic coercion undermine the general conditions upon which each person's agency relies.","A moral principle is objective when its application depends upon publicly assessable facts about agency and flourishing rather than upon a speaker's momentary approval or emotional intensity.","Sentient nonhuman animals can be moral patients without being moral agents because suffering, preference, and vulnerability provide life-relevant reasons for protection even when reciprocal responsibility is impossible."],"conclusion":"Therefore, facts about flourishing can support defeasible objective duties protecting agency, reciprocal freedom, and sentient welfare, although their precise scope requires continuing empirical and philosophical specification."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Constitutive agency test","text":"Instead of asking whether a murderer happens to profit on one occasion, evaluate the maxim authorizing that conduct as a principle for agents who depend upon stable recognition of planning, bodily security, evidence, and voluntary cooperation. A permission for advantageous killing selectively exempts the actor from conditions that make rational social agency possible. The objection is therefore not merely that murder usually backfires, but that opportunistic coercion contradicts the reciprocal framework the actor invokes whenever claiming authority over personal judgment and action."},{"title":"Patienthood without agency","text":"Moral agency and moral patienthood answer different questions: who can deliberate about principles, and whose condition supplies reasons for deliberators. Animals may lack responsibility while still possessing welfare, aversive experience, preferences, and vulnerability to purposeless injury. A life-centered framework can therefore condemn torture because it destroys an objectively identifiable good of another sentient organism for no proportionate agency-protecting purpose. This route retains distinctions between human and animal capacities without treating inability to reciprocate as permission for gratuitous suffering."}]},"con":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that moral discourse primarily coordinates and expresses deeply held attitudes, while factual reasoning informs those attitudes without creating stance-independent categorical authority.","premises":["Descriptions of survival, flourishing, agency, or suffering can identify consequences and instrumental relations, but no such description alone entails that every agent categorically ought to prioritize them.","Moral discourse characteristically expresses approval, condemnation, commitment, and demands for coordination, while remaining responsive to factual correction concerning the objects and consequences of those attitudes.","Persistent moral disagreement can remain after factual agreement because agents differ in ultimate attitudes, priorities, and emotional attachments, which supports an expressive account without proving every utterance purely emotive.","Rights can function as justified social conventions protecting shared interests and reducing coercive conflict even if they are not stance-independent properties that categorically bind every conceivable egoist."],"conclusion":"Therefore, emotivism can interpret moral claims as reason-responsive expressions and coordination commitments while denying that life, flourishing, or agency supplies universally categorical moral truth conditions."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Reason-responsive expressivism","text":"Expressive moral discourse need not exclude reasoning. Facts can reveal that a condemned practice does not cause the believed harm, that two commitments conflict, or that a policy frustrates values its supporters already endorse. Reason then improves coherence and instrumental accuracy without discovering a categorical property of oughtness. This explains why moral argument feels substantive: participants seek factual correction, consistency, imaginative identification, and coordinated commitment, even when their ultimate motivations remain affective and no stance-independent value compels every possible agent."},{"title":"Institutional rights without moral properties","text":"Rights may be defended as durable public rules that agents have powerful shared reasons to support: they reduce domination, stabilize expectations, protect planning, and permit peaceful disagreement. Their institutional justification does not require treating rights as metaphysical objects or pretending that every advantaged violator feels categorically bound. Enforcement addresses precisely those exceptional violators. This account preserves criticism, protection, and collective action while interpreting rights-language as a forceful public commitment grounded in convergent interests rather than as proof of objective moral properties."}]}},"year":2024,"topicCategory":"moral-realism-objectivity"};
